have n-2 non-sunny lines, and we already used one vertical line for column n-2, but they didn't account for the other columns. Actually they didn't need to allocate vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3 because those columns have many points, but they could be covered by other non-sunny lines (vertical lines) as well. But they didn't discuss that. So the argument is incomplete but the conclusion is correct.\n\nNow, the solution also argues that k≥4 is impossible. They say: \"Since a sunny line can meet each column in at most one point, having k≥4 would force at least four columns to rely on sunny lines for at least one of their points, leaving at most n-4 vertical lines to cover the remaining columns. Because the three rightmost columns already force the use of a vertical line for column n-2, we would run out of vertical lines before all columns are covered.\" This argument is also somewhat vague. Let's examine more systematically: For k≥4, we have n-k non-sunny lines. Since each column with size > k must have a vertical line. For k≥4, columns with size > k are columns 1,...,n-(k+1)? Actually column sizes are h_c = n+1-c. So column c has size n+1-c. Condition h_c > k => n+1-c > k => c n+1-k. So columns c = 1,...,n-k (since c integer). So we need vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-k. That's n-k columns. Non-sunny lines total = n-k. So we can allocate vertical lines for exactly those columns, using all non-sunny lines. Then columns n-k+1,...,n remain. Column n-k+1 has size k (since h_{n-k+1} = n+1-(n-k+1) = k). Column n-k+2 has size k-1, etc. So the remaining columns have sizes ≤ k. They can be covered by sunny lines (each column can have at most k points, and we have k sunny lines, each can cover at most one point per column). However, we need to ensure that the k sunny lines can cover all points in the remaining columns. The remaining columns are n-(n-k) = k columns: columns n-k+1,...,n. Their total number of points is sum_{i=1}^{k} i = k(k+1)/2. Each sunny line can 